<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Empire of Nicaea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1204">1204</a>–<a href="/years/1261">1261</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Nicene Empire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> was the largest of the three Greek rump states founded by the aristocracy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> after crusaders occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> in 1204. Established by the Laskaris family in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris">Theodore I Laskaris</a>, it assumed the traditional titles of the Byzantine emperors in 1205. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> recovered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Macedonia and incorporated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>. In July 1261 the general Alexios Strategopoulos entered Constantinople, restoring the empire under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>. By c. 1300 nearly the whole of the former Nicene lands had been conquered by the Turks.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>When crusaders invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204, the Byzantine emperor Alexios V Doukas Mourtzouphlos fled the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris">Theodore I Laskaris</a>, son-in-law of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-angelos">Alexios III Angelos</a>, was proclaimed emperor but also fled, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> had poor control of former Byzantine territory, and Greek successor states arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and Nicaea. Nicaea was closest to the Latin Empire and best placed to re-establish the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-flanders">Henry of Flanders</a> defeated Theodore at Poimanenon and Prusa in 1204, but Theodore captured much of northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> after the Bulgarian defeat of Latin Emperor Baldwin I at Adrianople recalled Henry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1205 Theodore assumed the traditional titles of the Byzantine emperors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1211 Theodore defeated a Seljuk invasion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Meander</a>. John III Doukas Vatatzes succeeded his father-in-law and secured his throne against the Laskarid brothers Isaac and Alexios at the Battle of Poimanenon. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 1224 and crowned himself emperor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> took control of that realm at Klokotnitsa in 1230. In 1246 John attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, recovered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Macedonia, and incorporated Thessalonica. By 1248 he had surrounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a>, and he went on taking land from it until his death in 1254.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-ii-laskaris">Theodore II Laskaris</a> defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>. After his death in 1258 the child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-laskaris">John IV Laskaris</a> came under the regency of Michael Palaiologos, who proclaimed himself co-emperor as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII</a> in 1259 and defeated an invasion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manfred-king-of-sicily">Manfred of Sicily</a>, the Despot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and the Latin Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a>. In July 1261 Alexios Strategopoulos persuaded the guards to open the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> while most of the Latin army was away, and Michael was recognised as emperor a few weeks later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> were stripped of their wealth to rebuild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and pay for wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> states and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. Soldiers moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, leaving the old frontier thinly defended, and Turkish raids went unchecked. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-laskaris">John IV Laskaris</a> was blinded on Michael's orders on 25 December 1261, which led the patriarch Arsenios Autoreianos to excommunicate Michael. By c. 1300 the Turks had conquered nearly all of it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a> fell in 1326, Nicaea in 1331 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> in 1337.</p><p>The court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> often used the term Hellenes of its Greek speaking population, and some scholars see rising Greek ethnic consciousness in it; others caution that the official ideology still held the state to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Empire of Nicaea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This brought the brothers into conflict with Theodore I Laskaris, who was building the rival <b>Empire of Nicaea</b> in western Anatolia, and David even sought help from the Latin emperor in Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The founding of the Latin Empire created five polities at once that claimed to be the Roman Empire: the Latin Empire, the Holy Roman Empire, the Despotate of Epirus, the <b>Empire of Nicaea</b> and the Empire of Trebizond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The following year, allied with Michael II of Epirus and Manfred of Sicily against the <b>Empire of Nicaea</b>, William was defeated and captured at the Battle of Pelagonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaloyan's union with Rome lapsed under Ivan Asen II, who allied with the <b>Empire of Nicaea</b>, and in 1235 a council at Lampsacus recognised the archbishop of Tarnovo as patriarch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaykhusraw I was killed in 1211 fighting the <b>Empire of Nicaea</b> at Antioch on the Meander, but his sons expanded to the coasts: Kaykaus I took Sinop and captured the emperor of Trebizond, and Kayqubad I fortified Alanya, building its Red Tower and a shipyard, sent an expedition…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>George Mouzalon, a high official of the <b>Empire of Nicaea</b> under Theodore II Laskaris and its regent, was assassinated in a conspiracy of nobles led by Michael VIII Palaiologos, who became co-emperor in 1259 and recovered Constantinople in 1261.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
